A||||6||wilburt||Fuel pressure regulator screws||||1||
Z||000000||wilburt||03-27-2000||11:33 PM||||Can anyone help me with the name of the tool used to remove\replace the fuel pressure regulator cover screws?<P>The stainless steel screws have what appear to be a T-10 Torx head pattern, with a 'pin' in the center.  The 'pin' prevents the insertation of a T-10 Torx driver.<P>Maybe someone knows a GM tool part number?<BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||03-27-2000||11:47 PM||||These are called "security Torx" the tools are available in a number of places. See if there is a tool store in your area or try the local tool truck. I believe you can also get these from <A HREF="http://www.jensentools.com/" TARGET=_blank>Jensen Tools</A> which is a large electronics tool supplier.<P>If you're sure they are T-10 and you're desperate then you can also get this tool from Compaq as part of the security screw kit for some Armada laptops. Contact your local Compaq authorized repair center and get the "security screw kit for Compaq Armada 7000 family laptops." It's in "quick finder." I just got one a couple weeks ago. ((I don't know what it costs. I ordered it as part of a laptop repair I was doing. Go to Jensen.))<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dan87GT||03-28-2000||07:17 AM||||I got a set from JC Whitney a fews week ago, I believe the set was around $15||||reg||1
Z||000003||wilburt||03-28-2000||08:48 AM||||Thanks fellas, I was about to get out the blow torch!!<P>I've been lurking this board for about a year now, and have learned enough to bring back two dead Fiero's.<P>My thanks to those that contribute to this forum, and I hope to gain enough knowledge to help someone here myself. <P>wilburt....'86 2M4, '87 GT.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||03-28-2000||01:30 PM||||Hmmm... Fuel regulator....  Blow torch....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Capt Fiero||03-28-2000||04:50 PM||||Sounds as though he is trying to make a real hot fiero to me!!!!!!!! LOL||||reg||1
Z||000006||WGraffiKs||03-28-2000||05:26 PM||||Unless you need to work on other security torx-protected areas, try this:  Take a small drift punch (or a blunt nail) and tap the center pin sideways.  It will break right off at the base.  Remove it, and use the standard torx tool.  We learned this trick when doing design work for GM.||||reg||1
